  "worked_solution": "Let ${d}_{1}$ and ${d}_{2}$ be the distances between the fulcrum and $A$ and $B$, respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/31dd97f05b7dde8459bf79efbebcac082571c49282aeb63f926b0c1c058923c8.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-8\\cdot {d}_{1}=-8{d}_{1}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $4\\cdot {d}_{2}=4{d}_{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe principle of moments tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nSo, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n4{d}_{2}-8{d}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe need to find ${d}_{2},$ so we eliminate ${d}_{1}.$\nUsing the fact that the rod has length $6\\,m,$ we have\n\n$$\n{d}_{1}+{d}_{2}=6\\,\u27f9\\,{d}_{1}=6-{d}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{d}_{2}-8(6-{d}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4{d}_{2}-48+8{d}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 12{d}_{2} & =48 \\\\ {d}_{2} & =4\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
